1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,320
Every sales team has the same leak.

2
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:04,643
Not bad products, not bad salespeople.

3
00:00:04,713 --> 00:00:07,800
Leads that go quiet, and nobody follows up
in time.

4
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:12,882
Let's look at a car dealership. Leads are
in the CRM,

5
00:00:12,952 --> 00:00:14,771
stock is tracked separately,

6
00:00:14,841 --> 00:00:18,181
and follow-ups depend on salespeople
checking both.

7
00:00:18,251 --> 00:00:20,764
When a customer's first choice sells,

8
00:00:20,834 --> 00:00:23,800
a suitable alternative can go unnoticed.

9
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:30,635
In our sample dealership, Maria asked
about a blue RAV4 Hybrid nine days ago.

10
00:00:30,705 --> 00:00:33,739
Her last contact was a week ago.

11
00:00:33,809 --> 00:00:36,376
Since then, that car has sold,

12
00:00:36,446 --> 00:00:42,280
while a silver alternative has been
sitting at the same dealership for 74

13
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:46,640
days. The dealership has an option worth
showing her.

14
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:51,800
But nobody has followed up to find out
whether she's still looking.

15
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:55,035
Nearly half of qualified leads are
mishandled.

16
00:00:55,105 --> 00:00:57,800
Most eventual buyers purchase within three
days.

17
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:03,800
Sales slip away when nobody connects the
lead to the right offer in time.

18
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:07,091
SalesDesk is your AI enterprise sales
analyst.

19
00:01:07,161 --> 00:01:11,800
It does that review overnight and prepares
the follow-ups for your team.

20
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:16,614
At 2 AM, it starts reviewing each store's
leads and inventory.

21
00:01:16,684 --> 00:01:20,800
Six stores run in parallel, in isolated
sandboxes on Vultr.

22
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:22,800
Let's look at Sandy.

23
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:28,905
SalesDesk reviews its 375 open leads and
flags 40 that need attention.

24
00:01:28,975 --> 00:01:32,430
Twenty-five have gone at least three days
without contact.

25
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:35,557
Others are waiting on a car that has sold,

26
00:01:35,627 --> 00:01:38,800
or have an appointment that needs
following up.

27
00:01:39,150 --> 00:01:41,362
It then checks the available inventory:

28
00:01:41,432 --> 00:01:43,640
117 cars at Sandy

29
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:46,800
and more than sixteen hundred at nearby
sister stores.

30
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:51,416
Each potential match is scored against
what the customer asked for,

31
00:01:51,486 --> 00:01:54,698
including the model, trim, budget and
mileage.

32
00:01:54,768 --> 00:01:58,800
Cars that have been in stock longer get
extra priority.

33
00:01:59,150 --> 00:02:01,911
Qwen, running on Vultr Serverless
Inference,

34
00:02:01,981 --> 00:02:05,313
uses those checked results to write
Sandy's morning brief

35
00:02:05,383 --> 00:02:08,800
and three follow-up drafts for its
priority leads.

36
00:02:09,150 --> 00:02:12,343
The recorded review finishes across all 24
stores

37
00:02:12,413 --> 00:02:14,800
in two minutes and seven seconds.

38
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:16,800
Here's Maria's result.

39
00:02:17,150 --> 00:02:22,005
The silver RAV4 is the same model and trim
she originally asked about.

40
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:27,800
It scores 98 out of 100, with the colour
difference shown in the breakdown.

41
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:30,989
The manager can see her original enquiry,

42
00:02:31,059 --> 00:02:33,202
when she was last contacted,

43
00:02:33,272 --> 00:02:37,800
and when the blue car sold, all in the
same view.

44
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:42,359
Below that is a draft message mentioning
the available silver RAV4

45
00:02:42,429 --> 00:02:46,430
and asking about her 2017 Honda CR-V
trade-in.

46
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,337
The salesperson has the context and a
message

47
00:02:49,407 --> 00:02:51,800
ready to review before contacting her.

48
00:02:52,150 --> 00:02:57,020
By the morning, Sandy's manager has a
ranked queue of 40 follow-ups.

49
00:02:57,090 --> 00:03:00,973
They can review individual drafts or
approve the queue together,

50
00:03:01,043 --> 00:03:04,800
then download the tasks and drafts for
their salespeople.

51
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:11,293
Across the group, 1,007 at-risk leads have
been matched to a car in stock.

52
00:03:11,363 --> 00:03:16,337
The estimated gross associated with those
matches is about $3.4 million.

53
00:03:16,407 --> 00:03:20,800
Managers can use that view to decide where
their teams should focus.

54
00:03:21,150 --> 00:03:25,372
The scheduler and analysis jobs run on a
Vultr VM.

55
00:03:25,442 --> 00:03:28,132
Qwen runs on Vultr Serverless Inference,

56
00:03:28,202 --> 00:03:32,800
and its output is validated before it
reaches the approval queue.

57
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:37,453
SalesDesk gives your team a practical
starting point each morning:

58
00:03:37,523 --> 00:03:40,063
which customers to follow up with,

59
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:44,800
which cars to offer, and a draft they can
work from.

60
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:48,800
SalesDesk. Your AI enterprise sales
analyst.
